  "agent": {
    "note": "**M1b — canvas camera (follow the active tile).** The canvas now **zooms in and pans to keep the active tile centered** as the geometry walk proceeds. New `cameraZoom` prop: `1` = fit-to-panel (the M1a behavior), `>1` = zoom + follow. This one is `1.6`.\n\nBuilt on a **new wired engine primitive** `effects.camera` (`{zoom, focusX, focusY}`, numbers or per-frame exprs) — it's ONE scale+crop on the finished canvas video, so the follow is **depth-independent** and works identically at 4 tiles or 400 (the large-layout case). Motion is a single eased (smoothstep) expression over the same timeline as the reveal + highlight, so everything stays frame-synced.\n\nInspector / DSL / Debugger panels hidden here to keep the focus on the canvas motion (toggles now work). Set `cameraZoom: 1` to compare against the fit-to-panel M1a.",
    "question": "Render it and watch the canvas: does the camera **ease to follow each tile** as it reveals (tile 1 → 2 → 3 → 4), framing the active one? Is `1.6` a good default zoom, or do you want more/less push-in? Any motion-feel notes (too fast/slow, snappy vs floaty) before I move to the inspector panel (M2)?",
    "context": {
      "category": {
        "kind": "other",
        "label": "canvas-camera"
      },
      "intent": {
        "action": "propose",
        "summary": "M1b — canvas camera that zooms + pans to follow the active tile"
      },
      "relation": {
        "kind": "refines",
        "target": "004"
      }
    },
    "response": {
      "body": "it does ease to each tile. i want a bit less zoom though. this particular layout needs NO zoom. we only zoom when the tiles dont cleanly fit into canvas. A good rule is at the current viewport, can i render every tile number and dimensions ok? if so, no zoom. if not, we need to zoom such that we see the most amount of tiles, but ALL have their frame number and dims painted well.",
